{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792192134,"nonce":"kGs9Ojq+gMu+bSJUUQzN7X2kU6k=","title":"LLVM tier refuses a dynamic call to a compiled function with nine arguments","message":"With --mode jit --jit-tier llvm, calling a static function that takes nine parameters through Dynamic or Reflect.callMethod raises 'Compiled call 30 has 9 args (max 8, no uniform entry)'. The default Cranelift tier, hybrid and the interpreter run the same program correctly (HashLink's own limit is nine arguments including this).\n\nReproducer: crates/ash/test/tests/TestDynCallManyArgs.hx (test_dyn_call_many_args.hl), after 2c09faa's stack-argument change: haxe -main TestDynCallManyArgs -hl x.hl; ash --mode jit --jit-tier llvm x.hl. Seen on main 8287782b too (the failing call is dyn static9 in the fixture), so it does not come from that change.\n\nThe message comes from the runtime bridge that enters an LLVM-compiled function by a generated uniform entry; a function wider than eight arguments gets none.","files":null}]}